@author "Claude (Anthropic)"
@design traditional
@source "[ida2020, Fig. 7.19]"

; The traditional crane, flat. The bird base is folded by two petal folds,
; each one flatten of two fans (ADR 0055); it is the state Ida's steps 2-10
; reach by reverse folds [ida2020, Fig. 7.19]. Her steps 11-17 follow. Her
; steps 18 and 19 open the wings, which is 3D and out of scope (ADR 0015).
; Checked against her figures in docs/notes/2026-09-25-crane-oracle.md.

paper square

fold (map .a onto .c) as --bd           ; triangle
reverse (map .b onto .c) as --h         ; preliminary base
reverse (map .d onto .c) as --v

.o = --h * --v                          ; the paper center
--mid = (through .c .o)                 ; center line of the base

.sr = --ab * --h                        ; the four side corners
.sl = --da * --v
.br = --bc * --h
.bl = --cd * --v

; the bird base: each petal fold opens the two halves of the side edges
; toward the side corners, brings the corners onto the center line and lifts
; the flap between them past the paper center. The legs .b and .d stay at
; the apex. Each fan binds the point where its kite crease meets the crease
; between the two fans.
flatten (fan (--h & .sr) (map .sr onto --mid through .a) as .e1)
        (fan (--v & .sl) (map .sl onto --mid through .a) as .e2)
        (on #[.a]) (staying .o) as --pf
flatten (fan (--h & .br) (map .br onto --mid through .c) as .e3)
        (fan (--v & .bl) (map .bl onto --mid through .c) as .e4)
        (on #[.c]) (staying .o) as --pb

; the head will bend toward a point on the edge of the front petal, read
; here while that edge is still one piece
.ht = free on --[.e2 .a] from .a at 1/3

; narrow the legs: each outer edge onto the center line, front layers as
; valleys, back layers as mountains. The outer edge of a leg is the kite
; crease from its fan's point to its tip. Each `up to` names the face of the
; leg that holds its anchor, the leg's tip and the paper center; without it
; the front fold takes the back layers too.
fold (map --[.e1 .b] onto --mid) (moving .e1) (up to #[.e1 .b .o]) as --n1
fold (map --[.e2 .d] onto --mid) (moving .e2) (up to #[.e2 .d .o]) as --n2
fold (map --[.e3 .b] onto --mid) (moving .e3) (up to #[.e3 .b .o]) (mountain) as --n3
fold (map --[.e4 .d] onto --mid) (moving .e4) (up to #[.e4 .d .o]) (mountain) as --n4

; neck and tail: each leg reversed about the perpendicular to its own
; narrowed edge through the point where the side corners landed. These are
; Ida's rays for steps 15 and 16 exactly. A narrowed leg opens at three
; places: between its two halves, and between its two outer layers and the
; six beside them, once on each side. The letters on its two narrowing
; creases keep the opening between its two halves.
.pn = free on --n1 & #[.sr .b] from .b at 1/4
.pn3 = free on --n3 & #[.br .b] from .b at 1/4
reverse (perp --n1 through .sr) (moving .b) (--n1 & .pn mountain) (--n3 & .pn3 mountain) as --neck
.pt = free on --n2 & #[.sl .d] from .d at 1/4
.pt4 = free on --n4 & #[.bl .d] from .d at 1/4
reverse (perp --n2 through .sr) (moving .d) (--n2 & .pt mountain) (--n4 & .pt4 mountain) as --tail

; head: a free choice in folding practice, so two free points carry it.
; .hp is where the head bends, .ht, bound above, sets its angle. Ida's ray
; for step 17 passes within 0.0032 of .ht and bends the neck at 0.2346 of
; its length from the tip.
.hp = free on --bd & .b from .b at 1/4
reverse (through .hp .ht) (moving .b) as --head

; the finished crane stands on its center line, the wing tip .c up
@orient .o .c up

; assert steps = 12
; assert faces = 60

; the two wings lie together at the far end, the paper center stays put
; assert .a = .c
; assert .o = (1/2, 1/2)

; the four side corners and the four fan points each share one point of
; the center line
; assert .sr = .sl
; assert .sr = .br
; assert .sr = .bl
; assert .e1 = .e2
; assert .e1 = .e3
; assert .e1 = .e4
